  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1227 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nThis morning's upper air analysis featured an upper-level ridge\ncentered over the Southeast U.S. with a longwave trough axis\nextending from the Northern Plains to the Southwest U.S.\nSouthwest flow aloft is present over portions of the\nCentral/Southern Plains and Middle/Lower Mississippi Valleys. A\nfew subtle embedded shortwaves are producing some showers and\nthunderstorms upstream along the I-40 corridor in the vicinity of\na cold front. Very warm and dry conditions persist across the Mid-\nSouth with late morning temperatures in the 80s.\n\nAnother hot afternoon is expected across the area as temperatures\nrise into the lower 90s across most locations. This will result\nin the potential for a few additional high temperature records to\nbe tied or broken. This period of hot weather will come to an end\nas a strong northern stream upper-level trough will drop into the\nregion later tonight into Friday. This will result in strong cold\nair advection producing substantially cooler temperatures for\nFriday and the upcoming weekend.  Highs this weekend will be\nmostly in the 60s with lows Saturday night falling into the upper\n30s to middle 40s.\n\nShort-term model trends and soundings continue to indicate very\nsparse moisture associated with this frontal passage. Thus,\nconfidence remains very low for any location receiving anything\nmore than perhaps a few hundredths of an inch of rain at best.\nElevated and gusty winds will accompany the frontal passage with\nsurface dewpoints dropping substantially Friday afternoon. These\nelevated winds and relative humidity values between 30-\n35 percent, generally for areas along and west of a line from\nDyersburg, TN to Oxford, MS, may result in an elevated fire\ndanger Friday afternoon and the issuance of a Rangeland Fire\nDanger statement.\n\nLong range deterministic and ensemble model trends continue to\nshow a dry pattern persisting across the region as an omega block\nsets up across the Southern U.S. next week. Consequently,\ntemperatures will transition back to above normal next week with\nhighs in the 80s towards the end of the period.",
